\par
\b\fs30 1. Executive summary\b0\fs24\par
\par
The short answer is this:\par
\par
SehatSaathi can become a real business, but it is unlikely to become an attractive business if it is launched primarily as a direct-to-consumer report-upload app that depends on paid user acquisition.\par
\par
The product becomes much more viable if it is positioned as a healthcare continuity layer that sits at the moment of report generation, report review, or chronic follow-up. In practice, that means labs, diagnostic networks, chronic care clinics, endocrine and nephrology follow-up practices, and hospital OPD front desks are the best entry points.\par
\par
The strongest truth in your favor is that the current product direction is no longer “generic AI health advice.” It has become something much more specific: a system that turns a lab report into a calmer follow-up journey, creates continuity between visits, and helps the patient arrive better prepared to the next clinical interaction.\par
\par
The strongest truth against you is that India is already crowded with large consumer health brands that can outspend you on acquisition, underprice you on tests, and bundle doctor consults, pharmacy, diagnostics, and wallets in a way a startup should not try to match directly.\par
\par
So the central founder question is not “Can this be built?” It already can. The real question is “Where does this sit in the value chain so that someone pays for it without the business burning cash?”\par
\par
My brutal answer:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab As a consumer acquisition business: weak to dangerous early.\par
\tab\bullet\tab As a B2B2C continuity workflow for labs and clinics: promising.\par
\tab\bullet\tab As a white-label continuity layer for diagnostics and outpatient follow-up: strongest near-term path.\par
\tab\bullet\tab As a trust-sensitive doctor-replacement or diagnosis engine: wrong path.\par
\par
This memo recommends that SehatSaathi should not enter the market by telling users “upload your report and we will explain your health.” That message is expensive to sell, hard to defend, and creates trust risk.\par
\par
Instead, SehatSaathi should enter by telling labs and clinics:\par
\par
“Your patients already receive reports. We help you turn that dead PDF into a calmer follow-up layer, better repeat retention, and cleaner continuity between visits.”\par
\par
That is a much stronger point of entry.\par
\par
