\b\fs30 2. What SehatSaathi actually is\b0\fs24\par
\par
If you describe the product incorrectly, everything after that breaks: legal positioning, GTM, CAC, trust, and pricing.\par
\par
SehatSaathi is not best understood as:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab an AI doctor\par
\tab\bullet\tab a diagnosis engine\par
\tab\bullet\tab a fitness app\par
\tab\bullet\tab a symptom tracker\par
\tab\bullet\tab a wellness planner\par
\tab\bullet\tab a lab marketplace\par
\par
It is best understood as:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab a health continuity layer\par
\tab\bullet\tab a report follow-up intelligence layer\par
\tab\bullet\tab a lab-to-patient-to-doctor bridge\par
\tab\bullet\tab a structured between-visits operating layer\par
\par
That matters because the business model changes depending on how it is described.\par
\par
If you market it as “AI diagnosis from reports,” you attract regulatory scrutiny, high user skepticism, doctor resistance, and medically risky expectations.\par
\par
If you market it as “a calm continuity layer that helps patients understand what deserves follow-up, what can wait, and how to prepare for review,” you create a much more defensible product category.\par
\par
The current product, based on the codebase and workflow now present, has five real assets:\par
\par
\b 1.\b0 A report extraction and normalization layer.\par
\b 2.\b0 A guided follow-up intelligence layer.\par
\b 3.\b0 A contextual action-plan layer.\par
\b 4.\b0 A retest timing and continuity layer.\par
\b 5.\b0 A lab/ops workflow that can deliver the report into the patient record directly.\par
\par
That is already more interesting than a plain summary app.\par
\par
