\b\fs30 3. Proof of concept: what has actually been proven vs what has not\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b\fs26 3.1 What is already proven in product terms\b0\fs24\par
\par
At a proof-of-concept level, SehatSaathi has already demonstrated a meaningful chain:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab report ingestion\par
\tab\bullet\tab extraction\par
\tab\bullet\tab deterministic understanding\par
\tab\bullet\tab calmer patient-facing follow-up interpretation\par
\tab\bullet\tab action plan generation\par
\tab\bullet\tab doctor handoff support\par
\tab\bullet\tab lab-side upload workflow\par
\tab\bullet\tab retest guidance\par
\par
That means the concept is not theoretical anymore. The core user journey exists.\par
\par
From a product thesis perspective, three important things are already proven:\par
\par
#### A. Patients do not just need “the report”\par
\par
They need:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab what matters first\par
\tab\bullet\tab what can wait\par
\tab\bullet\tab what to ask\par
\tab\bullet\tab what to carry into follow-up\par
\par
That is a real problem.\par
\par
#### B. Labs and clinics create a dead-end moment today\par
\par
Most reports become a PDF, WhatsApp image, portal login, or printout. After that, continuity is weak. The patient often receives a number, not a pathway.\par
\par
That gap is commercially meaningful because diagnostics providers want:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab better repeat behavior\par
\tab\bullet\tab higher retention\par
\tab\bullet\tab more trust\par
\tab\bullet\tab less confusion\par
\tab\bullet\tab more follow-up completion\par
\par
#### C. There is room between raw diagnostics and clinical consultation\par
\par
You do not need to replace the doctor to create value. You only need to reduce confusion and improve continuity.\par
\par
That is the most promising space in the stack.\par
\par
\b\fs26 3.2 What is not yet proven\b0\fs24\par
\par
You should be honest about the gaps:\par
\par
#### A. You have not yet proven mass user trust at scale\par
\par
A founder-led pilot can hide a lot because you can explain the product personally. Public scale cannot.\par
\par
#### B. You have not yet proven that patients return without being prompted\par
\par
Continuity is the product promise. If users only upload once and never return, the continuity thesis weakens.\par
\par
#### C. You have not yet proven that labs will pay for this rather than seeing it as a “nice extra”\par
\par
The core buyer question is not “is this good UX?” It is “does this improve retention, repeat testing, operational quality, or partner differentiation enough to justify spend?”\par
\par
#### D. You have not yet proven enterprise buying motion\par
\par
In Indian healthcare, product value and buying behavior are not the same thing. A product may be useful but still hard to sell if it sits outside the immediate revenue workflow.\par
\par
So the current state is:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab strong product proof of concept\par
\tab\bullet\tab partial operational proof\par
\tab\bullet\tab incomplete commercial proof\par
\par
That is normal. It just means you are at the beginning of company proof, not only product proof.\par
