\b\fs30 6. Point of entry: where this product should enter the market\b0\fs24\par
\par
This is the most important strategic section.\par
\par
\b\fs26 6.1 Direct-to-consumer as first entry: not recommended\b0\fs24\par
\par
A D2C-first launch sounds attractive because it feels scalable. In practice it creates four problems:\par
\par
\b 1.\b0 CAC is likely high.\par
\b 2.\b0 Trust is fragile because health products are judged harshly.\par
\b 3.\b0 The patient has little habit motivation after a single report event.\par
\b 4.\b0 Revenue per consumer is usually too low unless you become a transaction platform.\par
\par
This is exactly where many health startups burn money.\par
\par
\b\fs26 6.2 Best point of entry: independent labs and diagnostics groups\b0\fs24\par
\par
The best first entry point is likely independent labs and regional diagnostics chains.\par
\par
Why?\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab They already sit at the moment the report is created.\par
\tab\bullet\tab They want retention and repeat behavior.\par
\tab\bullet\tab They can deliver SehatSaathi at near-zero consumer acquisition cost.\par
\tab\bullet\tab They can use it as a trust and convenience differentiator.\par
\tab\bullet\tab Your lab workspace is already directionally aligned to this motion.\par
\par
The pitch becomes:\par
\par
“Instead of sending a PDF and losing the patient, give them a continuity layer that improves follow-up trust and keeps your brand present after the result.”\par
\par
That is much more commercial than asking end users to discover you alone.\par
\par
\b\fs26 6.3 Second best entry: chronic specialty clinics\b0\fs24\par
\par
The next best wedge is clinics with repeat-report behavior:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab diabetes/endocrine\par
\tab\bullet\tab nephrology\par
\tab\bullet\tab lipid/cardiometabolic follow-up\par
\tab\bullet\tab thyroid follow-up\par
\tab\bullet\tab allergy/asthma clinics where context matters\par
