\b\fs30 9. Business model options\b0\fs24\par
\par
There are five realistic revenue models. They are not equally good.\par
\par
\b\fs26 9.1 Model A: D2C subscription\b0\fs24\par
\par
Users upload reports and pay monthly or annually.\par
\par
Pros:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab simple story\par
\tab\bullet\tab clean unit economics on paper\par
\tab\bullet\tab direct relationship with user\par
\par
Cons:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab hard to acquire trust\par
\tab\bullet\tab difficult habit loop\par
\tab\bullet\tab low willingness to pay unless repeatedly sick or chronic\par
\tab\bullet\tab competing against free or bundled alternatives\par
\par
Verdict: weak as primary model.\par
\par
\b\fs26 9.2 Model B: Per-report fee charged to consumer\b0\fs24\par
\par
Users pay per uploaded report for summary + plan.\par
\par
Pros:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab simple\par
\tab\bullet\tab direct monetization\par
\par
Cons:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab high friction\par
\tab\bullet\tab low repeat value\par
\tab\bullet\tab hard to justify when consumer expects free explanation or WhatsApp help\par
\par
Verdict: not attractive as the main engine.\par
\par
\b\fs26 9.3 Model C: B2B2C lab SaaS / continuity fee\b0\fs24\par
\par
Labs pay a monthly SaaS fee, per-branch fee, or per-report usage fee.\par
\par
Pros:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab aligns with workflow\par
\tab\bullet\tab reduces consumer acquisition cost\par
\tab\bullet\tab easier recurring revenue\par
\tab\bullet\tab can improve partner retention and differentiation\par
\par
Cons:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab enterprise/SMB sales motion needed\par
\tab\bullet\tab buyer education required\par
\tab\bullet\tab must prove real workflow value\par
\par
Verdict: strongest early model.\par
\par
\b\fs26 9.4 Model D: Clinic / hospital follow-up workflow subscription\b0\fs24\par
\par
Clinics or hospitals pay for a continuity module.\par
\par
Pros:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab higher ACV potential\par
\tab\bullet\tab stronger clinical stickiness\par
\tab\bullet\tab better chronic-care use cases\par
\par
Cons:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab slower sales\par
\tab\bullet\tab more integration friction\par
\par
Verdict: strong second model after lab wedge.\par
\par
\b\fs26 9.5 Model E: White-label / enterprise licensing\b0\fs24\par
\par
You provide the continuity layer under partner branding.\par
\par
Pros:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab can unlock bigger institutions\par
\tab\bullet\tab good margin if product is mature\par
\par
Cons:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab longer cycles\par
\tab\bullet\tab custom demands\par
\tab\bullet\tab support complexity\par
\par
Verdict: later-stage scaling path.\par
\par
\b\fs26 9.6 Best model stack\b0\fs24\par
\par
Recommended revenue stack:\par
\par
\b 1.\b0 Primary: lab and clinic subscription or usage-based SaaS\par
\b 2.\b0 Secondary: enterprise white-label\par
\b 3.\b0 Tertiary: carefully governed teleconsult or retest partner monetization\par
\b 4.\b0 Avoid relying on D2C paid subscriptions early\par
\par
