\par
\b\fs30 12. Variable cost per report\b0\fs24\par
\par
This is where your model becomes interesting.\par
\par
\b\fs26 12.1 Current-state variable cost\b0\fs24\par
\par
Because the current report interpretation path is mostly deterministic after extraction, your variable cost per report can stay relatively low if:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab OCR costs are controlled\par
\tab\bullet\tab storage costs are reasonable\par
\tab\bullet\tab you do not use an expensive LLM for every report\par
\par
\b\fs26 12.2 Suggested modeled per-report cost range\b0\fs24\par
\par
Given current stack reality, a practical modeled range is:\par
\par
\f1 | Component | Low volume estimate | Mid estimate | High estimate |\f0\par
\f1 |---|---:|---:|---:|\f0\par
\f1 | OCR / extraction | INR 2 | INR 6 | INR 15 |\f0\par
\f1 | Storage + transfer + infra allocation | INR 1 | INR 3 | INR 6 |\f0\par
\f1 | Messaging allocation | INR 1 | INR 2 | INR 4 |\f0\par
\f1 | Optional OpenAI allocation | INR 0.5 | INR 3 | INR 10 |\f0\par
\f1 | Support / ops allocation | INR 2 | INR 5 | INR 10 |\f0\par
\f1 | **Estimated variable cost / report** | **INR 6.5** | **INR 19** | **INR 45** |\f0\par
\par
This is not a sourced market fact. It is a modeled cost framework based on:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab current code architecture\par
\tab\bullet\tab current cloud pricing references\par
\tab\bullet\tab the fact that Azure Document Intelligence bills per analyzed page\par
\tab\bullet\tab the fact that OpenAI usage can be kept optional or small\par
\par
\b\fs26 12.3 What kills margin\b0\fs24\par
\par
Your variable margin gets damaged if you:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab use premium LLM calls on every report unnecessarily\par
\tab\bullet\tab store and reprocess too much media carelessly\par
\tab\bullet\tab trigger repeat extraction often\par
\tab\bullet\tab support too many low-quality uploads manually\par
\par
\b\fs26 12.4 What protects margin\b0\fs24\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab deterministic report logic\par
\tab\bullet\tab OCR confidence gating\par
\tab\bullet\tab background processing\par
\tab\bullet\tab lab-side upload of cleaner documents\par
\tab\bullet\tab B2B2C distribution instead of high-CAC D2C\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 13. Financial models\b0\fs24\par
\par
These are scenario models, not certainty claims.\par
\par
\b\fs26 13.1 Model 1: D2C subscription model\b0\fs24\par
\par
Assumptions:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab monthly price: INR 149\par
\tab\bullet\tab active paying users: 1,000\par
\tab\bullet\tab monthly revenue: INR 1,49,000\par
\tab\bullet\tab user acquisition via paid channels\par
\tab\bullet\tab effective CAC often likely INR 300 to INR 1,500+ depending on channel and conversion\par
\par
This gets ugly fast.\par
\par
If CAC is even INR 500 and conversion retention is weak, you are likely underwater unless you have unusually strong retention or cross-sell.\par
\par
Conclusion:\par
\par
Pure D2C is financially dangerous early.\par
\par
\b\fs26 13.2 Model 2: Per-report consumer fee\b0\fs24\par
\par
Assumptions:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab INR 49 per report\par
\tab\bullet\tab 5,000 paid reports/month\par
\tab\bullet\tab gross revenue: INR 2,45,000\par
\tab\bullet\tab variable cost at INR 19/report: INR 95,000\par
\tab\bullet\tab contribution before fixed cost: INR 1,50,000\par
\par
Looks okay on paper, but the conversion challenge is severe. Many users will hesitate to pay for something they think should be free or bundled.\par
\par
Conclusion:\par
\par
Not the best early model.\par
\par
\b\fs26 13.3 Model 3: Lab SaaS model\b0\fs24\par
\par
Assumptions:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab 20 labs or branches\par
\tab\bullet\tab average monthly fee: INR 12,000\par
\tab\bullet\tab revenue: INR 2,40,000 / month\par
\par
At 50 labs or branches:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab revenue: INR 6,00,000 / month\par
\par
At 100 labs or branches:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab revenue: INR 12,00,000 / month\par
\par
This becomes meaningful because partner distribution lowers acquisition friction per end user.\par
\par
\b\fs26 13.4 Model 4: Usage-based lab model\b0\fs24\par
\par
Assumptions:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab 30,000 reports/month\par
\tab\bullet\tab partner price: INR 12/report\par
\tab\bullet\tab revenue: INR 3,60,000\par
\tab\bullet\tab variable cost at INR 19/report would be bad\par
\tab\bullet\tab variable cost at optimized INR 8-12/report becomes viable\par
\par
This model is workable only if you keep costs lean and automate aggressively.\par
\par
\b\fs26 13.5 Model 5: Hybrid subscription + usage\b0\fs24\par
\par
This is probably the strongest.\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab base platform fee: INR 8,000/month per lab\par
\tab\bullet\tab plus INR 5/report above included threshold\par
\par
This creates:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab predictable base revenue\par
\tab\bullet\tab upside with volume\par
\tab\bullet\tab more reasonable alignment for both sides\par
\par
