\par
\b\fs30 15. Marketing and distribution strategy\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b\fs26 15.1 What not to do\b0\fs24\par
\par
Do not start with broad consumer performance marketing.\par
\par
Why:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab health trust is expensive to buy\par
\tab\bullet\tab attribution is noisy\par
\tab\bullet\tab retention is uncertain\par
\tab\bullet\tab big incumbents already own mindshare\par
\par
\b\fs26 15.2 Recommended marketing posture\b0\fs24\par
\par
Your best early marketing is distribution-led, not ad-led.\par
\par
Priority channels:\par
\par
\b 1.\b0 diagnostic lab partnerships\par
\b 2.\b0 clinic pilots\par
\b 3.\b0 hospital follow-up champions\par
\b 4.\b0 founder-led demos\par
\b 5.\b0 WhatsApp and referral loops from partner facilities\par
\par
\b\fs26 15.3 What your first marketing should look like\b0\fs24\par
\par
Not mass campaigns. Instead:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab one-page partner deck\par
\tab\bullet\tab patient poster\par
\tab\bullet\tab front-desk explainer script\par
\tab\bullet\tab 2-minute demo\par
\tab\bullet\tab WhatsApp intro\par
\tab\bullet\tab field pilot walkthrough\par
\par
\b\fs26 15.4 Marketing spend recommendation\b0\fs24\par
\par
For first 6-12 months:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab keep paid consumer marketing low\par
\tab\bullet\tab keep spend on sales enablement, demos, collateral, and partner onboarding\par
\par
Suggested early monthly spend:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab INR 10,000 to INR 40,000 for materials and local promotion\par
\tab\bullet\tab not INR 2 lakh to INR 5 lakh on speculative consumer ads\par
\par
That discipline protects survival.\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 16. Why D2C user acquisition is probably a trap here\b0\fs24\par
\par
You already see this correctly.\par
\par
Reasons:\par
\par
\b\fs26 A. The moment of need is episodic\b0\fs24\par
\par
People do not wake up daily wanting a lab-report continuity app.\par
\par
\b\fs26 B. Consumer willingness to pay is low unless the value is repeatedly felt\b0\fs24\par
\par
One upload is not enough.\par
\par
\b\fs26 C. Search and ad markets are crowded\b0\fs24\par
\par
You compete indirectly with giant players for attention.\par
\par
\b\fs26 D. Trust costs money\b0\fs24\par
\par
Healthcare is not like a casual productivity app. Confused users churn faster and complain harder.\par
\par
\b\fs26 E. Your most efficient distribution already exists elsewhere\b0\fs24\par
\par
The lab, clinic, or hospital is the best acquisition surface because the report moment already exists there.\par
\par
So yes: if you go straight into paid D2C acquisition, it can burn money very fast.\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 17. Recommended entry strategy\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b\fs26 Phase 1: founder-led pilot\b0\fs24\par
\par
Target:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab 5 to 10 labs / clinics\par
\tab\bullet\tab 50 to 300 patients\par
\tab\bullet\tab 1 to 3 specialties or report clusters\par
\par
Goals:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab prove operational reliability\par
\tab\bullet\tab prove trust\par
\tab\bullet\tab prove repeat usage\par
\tab\bullet\tab prove one commercial metric partners care about\par
\par
That metric could be:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab repeat patient engagement\par
\tab\bullet\tab report open rate\par
\tab\bullet\tab follow-up completion\par
\tab\bullet\tab repeat testing retention\par
\tab\bullet\tab lower patient confusion load\par
\par
\b\fs26 Phase 2: convert to paid pilot or small subscription\b0\fs24\par
\par
Do not keep pilots free too long.\par
\par
Even a small fee changes seriousness.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Phase 3: productize for repeatable partner onboarding\b0\fs24\par
\par
By this stage you should know:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab which labs buy\par
\tab\bullet\tab which message closes\par
\tab\bullet\tab what the real onboarding friction is\par
\tab\bullet\tab which features are must-have vs nice-to-have\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 18. What could make this meaningfully profitable\b0\fs24\par
\par
Three conditions matter most.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Condition 1: stay out of high-CAC consumer acquisition early\b0\fs24\par
\par
This is the biggest strategic guardrail.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Condition 2: keep variable cost per report low\b0\fs24\par
\par
Current deterministic architecture is a good sign here.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Condition 3: sell workflow value, not AI novelty\b0\fs24\par
\par
If partners buy it because it is “AI,” the deal is fragile. If they buy it because it improves continuity and retention, the deal is stronger.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Profitability summary\b0\fs24\par
\par
Yes, SehatSaathi can absolutely become profitable.\par
\par
But the profitable version is:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab workflow software\par
\tab\bullet\tab continuity infrastructure\par
\tab\bullet\tab partner-distributed\par
\tab\bullet\tab clinically restrained\par
\par
The unprofitable version is:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab consumer-acquired\par
\tab\bullet\tab diagnosis-adjacent\par
\tab\bullet\tab feature-heavy\par
\tab\bullet\tab low willingness-to-pay\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 19. Biggest risks\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b\fs26 19.1 Product risk\b0\fs24\par
\par
If summaries ever feel overconfident, trust can collapse.\par
\par
\b\fs26 19.2 Distribution risk\b0\fs24\par
\par
If labs do not see commercial value, the wedge weakens.\par
\par
\b\fs26 19.3 Sales-cycle risk\b0\fs24\par
\par
Healthcare buyers can be slow. Cash planning matters.\par
\par
\b\fs26 19.4 Compliance risk\b0\fs24\par
\par
As data volume and partner sophistication grow, casual compliance will stop being enough.\par
\par
\b\fs26 19.5 Positioning risk\b0\fs24\par
\par
If you present this as doctor replacement or “AI diagnosis,” you increase resistance from every stakeholder.\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 20. Recommended next 12 months\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b\fs26 0 to 3 months\b0\fs24\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab prove lab and clinic pilot outcomes\par
\tab\bullet\tab tighten partner onboarding\par
\tab\bullet\tab collect case studies\par
\tab\bullet\tab validate paid willingness\par
\par
\b\fs26 3 to 6 months\b0\fs24\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab standardize partner plan\par
\tab\bullet\tab improve dashboards and audit trail\par
\tab\bullet\tab sharpen enterprise trust materials\par
\tab\bullet\tab add light commercial analytics\par
\par
\b\fs26 6 to 12 months\b0\fs24\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab regional cluster expansion\par
\tab\bullet\tab white-label discussions\par
\tab\bullet\tab deeper ABDM-aligned interoperability if needed\par
\tab\bullet\tab carefully add premium AI only where it improves outcomes, not just impresses demos\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 21. Final brutal verdict\b0\fs24\par
\par
Here is the blunt founder answer.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Is this a real problem?\b0\fs24\par
\par
Yes.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Is the product direction meaningful?\b0\fs24\par
\par
Yes.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Is it differentiated enough to matter?\b0\fs24\par
\par
Potentially yes, if framed as continuity infrastructure and not just “AI report summary.”\par
\par
\b\fs26 Can it be profitable?\b0\fs24\par
\par
Yes, if sold through labs, clinics, and follow-up workflows.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Can it be profitable as a D2C paid-acquisition business?\b0\fs24\par
\par
Probably not early, or at least not without painful burn and low odds.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Should you chase consumer scale first?\b0\fs24\par
\par
No.\par
\par
\b\fs26 Should you chase partner-distributed continuity first?\b0\fs24\par
\par
Yes.\par
\par
\b\fs26 My clearest strategic recommendation\b0\fs24\par
\par
SehatSaathi should enter the market as:\par
\par
“A continuity and follow-up layer for diagnostics and outpatient care.”\par
\par
Not as:\par
\par
“An AI app that explains reports.”\par
\par
That one positioning decision changes your odds materially.\par
\par
If you execute the right wedge, stay medically restrained, avoid expensive consumer acquisition, and sell measurable workflow value, this can become a healthy business.\par
\par
If you ignore that and try to win attention as another consumer health AI app, you will likely spend a lot of money teaching the market something that is hard to monetize.\par
\par
That is the brutal truth.\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 22. Recommended business decision\b0\fs24\par
\par
If I were advising you as a mentor, I would say:\par
\par
\b 1.\b0 Launch as a founder-led B2B2C pilot.\par
\b 2.\b0 Sell to labs and chronic-care follow-up settings first.\par
\b 3.\b0 Keep D2C as a brand layer, not the engine.\par
\b 4.\b0 Measure retention, repeat review behavior, and partner stickiness.\par
\b 5.\b0 Keep the AI restrained and the workflow strong.\par
\b 6.\b0 Do not burn money on early consumer acquisition unless the partner model fails clearly.\par
\par
That is the highest-probability path from product to business.\par
\par
