\b\fs30 25. One-page founder conclusion\b0\fs24\par
\par
If you remember only one thing from this memo, remember this:\par
\par
SehatSaathi is not strongest as a consumer app asking people to upload reports.\par
\par
SehatSaathi is strongest as the continuity layer that sits where the report is born, where confusion begins, and where the next visit is not yet organized.\par
\par
That is the real company.\par
\par
\par_______________________________________________________________\par
\par
\b\fs30 26. Detailed point-of-entry playbooks\b0\fs24\par
\par
This section goes one level deeper into how SehatSaathi should actually enter each segment.\par
\par
\b\fs26 26.1 Independent labs\b0\fs24\par
\par
This is the most practical wedge because the lab already owns:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab patient intake moment\par
\tab\bullet\tab report generation moment\par
\tab\bullet\tab repeat testing behavior\par
\tab\bullet\tab WhatsApp/SMS/email distribution surface\par
\par
#### Lab buyer pain points\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab Patients receive the report but do not always understand what matters.\par
\tab\bullet\tab Labs become “commodity PDF providers.”\par
\tab\bullet\tab Follow-up is not owned.\par
\tab\bullet\tab Repeat retention is often price- and convenience-driven.\par
\tab\bullet\tab Front-desk and support teams answer repetitive confusion questions.\par
\par
#### SehatSaathi pitch to labs\par
\par
“Give every report a continuity layer. Patients receive not only the result, but a calmer next-step surface that improves trust, helps them prepare for follow-up, and keeps your brand relevant after the report is delivered.”\par
\par
#### Why labs may say yes\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab better patient experience without becoming a doctor\par
\tab\bullet\tab modern digital differentiation\par
\tab\bullet\tab possible repeat retention improvement\par
\tab\bullet\tab more structured patient data continuity\par
\tab\bullet\tab future ABDM and digital-record readiness story\par
\par
#### Why labs may still hesitate\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab “Will this increase medico-legal risk?”\par
\tab\bullet\tab “Will patients misunderstand it as diagnosis?”\par
\tab\bullet\tab “Will this slow my workflow?”\par
\tab\bullet\tab “Will this improve revenue enough to matter?”\par
\tab\bullet\tab “Do I need another software vendor?”\par
\par
#### What closes the deal\par
\par
Not AI language. Not fancy visuals. Not product philosophy.\par
\par
What closes the deal is:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab no extra staff burden\par
\tab\bullet\tab no disruption to report delivery\par
\tab\bullet\tab measurable patient engagement\par
\tab\bullet\tab clear guardrails\par
\tab\bullet\tab low-friction onboarding\par
\par
\b\fs26 26.2 Chronic specialty clinics\b0\fs24\par
\par
Chronic clinics are powerful because the report is not isolated. It belongs to a larger story:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab diabetes care\par
\tab\bullet\tab thyroid monitoring\par
\tab\bullet\tab lipid management\par
\tab\bullet\tab kidney follow-up\par
\tab\bullet\tab anemia workup or repeat CBC review\par
\par
#### Clinic buyer pain points\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab patients forget prior reports\par
\tab\bullet\tab patients ask broad “is this serious?” questions without context\par
\tab\bullet\tab follow-up conversations begin from confusion instead of continuity\par
\tab\bullet\tab staff spends time organizing non-clinical noise\par
\par
#### SehatSaathi pitch to clinics\par
\par
“This is a pre-review preparation layer. It helps patients arrive with the important findings, better questions, and cleaner continuity between visits.”\par
\par
#### What makes this attractive\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab repeated report cycles\par
\tab\bullet\tab repeated visit cycles\par
\tab\bullet\tab repeated clinical interpretation need\par
\tab\bullet\tab higher lifetime value per patient\par
\par
#### What makes this harder\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab clinics may prefer to keep communication tightly doctor-led\par
\tab\bullet\tab smaller clinics may resist another tool unless it clearly saves time\par
\par
\b\fs26 26.3 Hospitals\b0\fs24\par
\par
Hospitals can be high-value but slower.\par
\par
Good fits:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab OPD follow-up programs\par
\tab\bullet\tab preventive health packages\par
\tab\bullet\tab medicine/endocrine/cardiology follow-up clinics\par
\tab\bullet\tab discharge-linked chronic care continuity\par
\par
#### Why hospitals are attractive\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab larger volume\par
\tab\bullet\tab stronger institutional trust\par
\tab\bullet\tab possibility of cross-department continuity\par
\tab\bullet\tab bigger contract value\par
\par
#### Why hospitals are difficult\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab slow procurement\par
\tab\bullet\tab many decision-makers\par
\tab\bullet\tab compliance scrutiny\par
\tab\bullet\tab integration demands\par
\par
Recommendation:\par
\par
Do not start here unless you already have a champion inside.\par
\par
\b\fs26 26.4 Pharmacies, employers, insurers\b0\fs24\par
\par
These are real possibilities, but not best first moves.\par
\par
#### Pharmacies\par
\par
Could support chronic refill continuity, but they do not own the report moment as cleanly.\par
\par
#### Employers\par
\par
Interesting if tied to annual health checkups, but enterprise wellness is noisy and often cost-sensitive.\par
\par
#### Insurers\par
\par
Possible long term for claims-adjacent continuity or preventive engagement, but much slower and more regulated.\par
\par
