\b\fs30 33. What the moat could actually become\b0\fs24\par
\par
At this stage, you do not have a classic hard moat. That is normal.\par
\par
Early-stage founders often lie to themselves about moat. The truth is that in the beginning, the “moat” is usually some combination of:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab execution speed\par
\tab\bullet\tab product sharpness\par
\tab\bullet\tab workflow fit\par
\tab\bullet\tab customer intimacy\par
\tab\bullet\tab trust\par
\par
That is true here as well.\par
\par
\b\fs26 33.1 Weak moats you should not overestimate\b0\fs24\par
\par
These are not durable enough on their own:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab “we use AI”\par
\tab\bullet\tab “we summarize reports”\par
\tab\bullet\tab “we have a health app”\par
\tab\bullet\tab “we have one nice UI layer”\par
\tab\bullet\tab “we support ABHA”\par
\par
Big players can imitate those quickly if they care.\par
\par
\b\fs26 33.2 Stronger moats you could build over time\b0\fs24\par
\par
#### A. Workflow embedment\par
\par
If SehatSaathi becomes part of the daily operating workflow of:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab lab front desk\par
\tab\bullet\tab report release\par
\tab\bullet\tab patient follow-up prep\par
\tab\bullet\tab doctor handoff\par
\par
then it becomes harder to remove.\par
\par
Workflow moat is more real than AI moat.\par
\par
#### B. Continuity graph\par
\par
If your product becomes the place where:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab prior reports are compared\par
\tab\bullet\tab follow-up questions accumulate\par
\tab\bullet\tab next-step history compounds\par
\tab\bullet\tab patient context becomes longitudinal\par
\par
then you own something deeper than a one-time summary: continuity memory.\par
\par
That is valuable.\par
\par
#### C. Trust moat\par
\par
In Indian health-tech, trust compounds slowly but matters enormously.\par
\par
If patients and partners learn that SehatSaathi is:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab calm\par
\tab\bullet\tab medically restrained\par
\tab\bullet\tab privacy-conscious\par
\tab\bullet\tab consistently useful\par
\par
then you can win a category that more aggressive but less careful competitors damage.\par
\par
#### D. Partner distribution network\par
\par
If enough labs or clinics use the product, distribution itself becomes a moat:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab lower acquisition cost\par
\tab\bullet\tab stronger data continuity\par
\tab\bullet\tab better case-study engine\par
\tab\bullet\tab more proof for the next buyer\par
\par
#### E. Report understanding + partner workflow + patient continuity together\par
\par
Any one feature is easy to copy.\par
\par
The full system is harder:\par
\par
\tab\bullet\tab extract\par
\tab\bullet\tab normalize\par
\tab\bullet\tab personalize\par
\tab\bullet\tab hand off\par
\tab\bullet\tab retest\par
\tab\bullet\tab retain continuity\par
\par
That integrated loop is more defensible than a standalone explainer.\par
\par
\b\fs26 33.3 What your moat should become in three years\b0\fs24\par
\par
The ideal future moat is not “best AI report summary.”\par
\par
It is:\par
\par
“the most trusted continuity layer between diagnostics and follow-up in India.”\par
\par
That is a much harder thing to copy, because it includes product, trust, workflow, relationships, and historical continuity.\par
